{"ex-408-1":{"number":"1","title":"Turns ratio and voltage ratio","question":"<p>The primary coil of a transformer is connected to the 220-V mains. There are 2200 turns in the primary coil and 110 turns in the secondary coil.</p><p>(a) What is the voltage across the secondary coil <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a2da029bc25c391c6a02df27.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:150.6px\"/></p><p>(b) If the primary coil is connected to a steady d.c. supply of 20 V, what will be the voltage across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 220 = 11 V</p><p>(b) 0</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The magnetic field produced by the primary coil will be steady, so no e.m.f. is induced in the secondary coil.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 4","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":2},"id":"ex-408-1","chapter":"408"},"ex-408-2":{"number":"2","title":"A multi-tapped transformer","question":"<p>The primary coil of a transformer is connected to the 220-V mains. There are 2200 turns in the primary coil. The secondary coil is tapped such that the number of turns across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are 30 turns, 40 turns and 50 turns</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/98a6885e6f8545df91ea87bb.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:210.3px\"/></p><p>respectively.</p><p>(a) What is the voltage across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p>(b) A device works at 7 V. Which terminals should this device be connected to?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 220 = 3 V</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 2200 = 70</p><p>∴ The device should be connected to terminals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Consider the part across AB in the secondary coil.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Check out across which two taps the number of turns is 70.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 6","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":15},"id":"ex-408-2","chapter":"408"},"ex-408-3":{"number":"3","title":"Efficiency of a transformer","question":"<p>A light bulb is connected to the 220-V mains via a transformer. The turns ratio of the transformer is 11 : 1. The power consumed by the bulb is 50 W. The primary current is 0.3 A.</p><p>(a) Find the secondary voltage.</p><p>(b) Find the secondary current.</p><p>(c) Find the power output of the mains.</p><p>(d) Find the efficiency of the transformer.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7f30f81a74b4d3e8d45d4820.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:194.6px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 220 = 20 V</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2.5 A</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = (220)(0.3) = 66 W</p><p>(d) Efficiency = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = 75.8%</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 9","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":32},"id":"ex-408-3","chapter":"408"},"ex-408-4":{"number":"4","title":"Ideal and practical transformers","question":"<p>A load rated at ‘12 V, 90 W’ is connected to the 220-V mains via a transformer. The primary coil has 4000 turns. The secondary coil has 200 turns.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/0979ec62c38d7200c6e7d6cf.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:395.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Suppose the transformer is ideal.</p><p>(i) Find the secondary current.</p><p>(ii) Find the primary current.</p><p>(b) Suppose the efficiency of the transformer is 50%.</p><p>(i) Find the secondary current.</p><p>(ii) Find the primary current.</p><p>(c) Suggest and explain one method to improve the efficiency of a practical transformer.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 220 = 11 V</p><p>Resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of load = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.6 Ω</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6.88 A</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 6.88 = 0.344 A</p><p>(b) (i) 6.88 A</p><p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × efficiency = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p><p>220<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> × 50% = (11)(6.88)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 0.688 A</p><p>(c) Any one of the following:</p><p>Use a soft iron core to reduce the energy loss in magnetizing and demagnetizing the core.</p><p>Use a laminated core to reduce eddy currents in the core.</p><p>Use thicker wires for the coils to reduce the resistance of the coils.</p><p>Exercise 8.1 Q4 (p.334)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The load does not work at its rated voltage.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>R can be found using the rated values.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Since the turns ratio and primary voltage remain unchanged, the secondary voltage, hence the secondary current, also remain unchanged.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 11","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":45},"id":"ex-408-4","chapter":"408"},"ex-408-5":{"number":"5","title":"Power loss in transmission cables","question":"<p>Electrical power of 4 kW is transmitted from a power supply to a load at voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> via transmission cables with a total resistance of 5 Ω. Find the percentage of power loss during transmission if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is (a) 100 V, (b) 2 kV.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/16c6ebdec0770613702f0779.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:352.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Current through cables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 10 A</p><p>Power loss in cables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (10)<sup>2</sup>(5) = 500 W</p><p>Percentage of power loss = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = 12.5%</p><p>(b) Current through cables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2 A</p><p>Power loss in cables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (2)<sup>2</sup>(5) = 20 W</p><p>Percentage of power loss = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = 0.5%</p>","tips":"<p>Editorial check</p><p>Part (a) states 100 V, but the printed solution substitutes 400 V. Using the stated 100 V gives a current of 40 A and a calculated cable loss of 8000 W (200% of 4000 W), showing that the stated operating conditions are inconsistent. The printed solution is retained above.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 17","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":77},"id":"ex-408-5","chapter":"408"},"ex-408-6":{"number":"6","title":"A power transmission system","question":"<p>The figure shows a simplified power transmission system. Each transmission cable is 20 km long and its resistance is 0.1 Ω per km. Transformers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are ideal. The voltage and power output of transformer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are 132 kV and 50 MW respectively. The turns ratio of transformer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 3 : 1.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1d4c535710c1a6dcf2213b11.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:447.2px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the p.d. across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find the total power loss in the transmission cables.</p><p>(c) Find the efficiency of this transmission system.</p><p>(d) Find the p.d. across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(e) Suggest two ways to increase the efficiency of this transmission system.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Consider transformer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>N</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 132 × 10<sup>3</sup> = 396 × 10<sup>3</sup> V = 396 kV</p><p>(b) Since transformer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is ideal,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 50 MW</p><p>Current through cables = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 126.3 A</p><p>Power loss in each cable = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (126.3)<sup>2</sup>(0.1 × 20) = 31 900 W</p><p>Total power loss in cables = 2 × 31 900 = 63 800 W</p><p>(c) Power input to system = 50 × 10<sup>6</sup> + 63 800 = 5.01 × 10<sup>7</sup> W</p><p>Efficiency of transmission system = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 100% = 99.8%</p><p>(d) Voltage drop across each cable = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>p</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (126.3)(0.1 × 20) = 253 V</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>V</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 396 × 10<sup>3</sup> + 253 + 253 = 397 × 10<sup>3</sup> V = 397 kV</p><p>(e) Transmit the power through the cables at a higher voltage.</p><p>Use cables of lower resistance.</p><p>Exercise 8.2 Q4 (p.340)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a967a6d3ff773155e4b47f00.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:139.0px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 4  |  Chapter 408  |  Source PDF p. 19","source":{"name":"408 Examples.docx","sha256":"5a154e96ef0dd9c6de738659bb45031447938003b5cddfcb48e237a3523f4c29","block":90},"id":"ex-408-6","chapter":"408"}}